{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And he knew what his younger son had done to him.</b> It is not written in the passage who told Noah what his son had done to him — did Noah see this through prophecy? And it appears to me according to what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 108b) that three cohabited in the ark: the raven, the dog, and Ham, etc. And according to this, when Noah arose from his sleep, he pondered within himself who had done this thing to him — surely one of his sons suspected of forbidden relations had done this to him, that he sodomized him. And out of his reflection he recalled the act that had occurred, and he knew what his younger son had done to him in the ark, for he had defiled him through intercourse at a forbidden time. Noah said: whoever did this, did also that — and therefore he cursed him. And as for why he cursed Canaan his son and did not curse Ham himself, opinions proliferate on this: for some say that Canaan saw and told his father Ham. And some say that since the Holy One, blessed be He, had blessed Noah and his sons, it is not fitting to curse one of his sons after HaShem had already blessed them. And some say that Canaan was one who uncovered nakedness, and his father learned from his son to do as Canaan did." "And the reason he cursed him with servitude is that one who consorts with harlots destroys wealth, and against his will he will become a slave for a morsel of bread, as they said of blessed memory (Sotah 4a): everyone who lies with a harlot ultimately seeks a loaf of bread and does not find it, etc. And as for what is written, ״עֶבֶד עֲבָדִים יִהְיֶה לְאֶחָיו״ (Genesis 9:25), and afterward he returned a second time and said ״וִיהִי כְנַעַן עֶבֶד לָמוֹ״ (Genesis 9:27) and did not mention 'slave of slaves' — it appears to me that 'a slave of slaves shall he be to his brothers' means that Canaan would be literally a slave of slaves to his brothers, the sons of Ham, for without this, a younger brother is already subordinate to his older brothers, and through the curse it was added that he should be to them a slave of slaves. But to Shem and to Japheth he would be simply 'a slave to them' — for this suffices, since without this he would not have needed to be a slave to his uncles."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:53.424562342Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}